This is the direct brute-force version. There are `n - 1` gaps between characters, and each gap can either contain a cut or not. I represent those choices with a bit mask, build the pieces for that mask, and keep the mask only when every piece is a palindrome.

It is slower than pruning while building the answer, but the code is easy to compare with the definition of the problem. The original string is left untouched.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> partition(String s) {
        List<List<String>> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        int n = s.length();

        if (n == 0) {
            answer.add(new ArrayList<>());
            return answer;
        }

        int numberOfMasks = 1 << (n - 1);
        for (int mask = 0; mask < numberOfMasks; mask++) {
            List<String> current = new ArrayList<>();
            int start = 0;
            boolean valid = true;

            for (int gap = 0; gap < n - 1; gap++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << gap)) != 0) {
                    String piece = s.substring(start, gap + 1);
                    if (!isPalindrome(piece)) {
                        valid = false;
                        break;
                    }
                    current.add(piece);
                    start = gap + 1;
                }
            }

            if (valid) {
                String lastPiece = s.substring(start);
                if (isPalindrome(lastPiece)) {
                    current.add(lastPiece);
                    answer.add(current);
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }

    private boolean isPalindrome(String value) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = value.length() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            if (value.charAt(left) != value.charAt(right)) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

An edge case such as `"a"` has no gaps, so there is one mask. The final piece is `"a"`, which is accepted. For a string with all different characters, only the mask that cuts after every character survives.

There are `2^(n-1)` masks. Building and checking one mask can take `O(n^2)`, so the total time is `O(n^2 * 2^n)`. The working space is `O(n)`, excluding the result list.

Instead of carrying one changing list through the recursion, this version asks for all partitions of each suffix and stores the result for that suffix. If the same suffix appears again, its answer is reused.

The helper tries every prefix. A prefix is accepted when it equals its reverse, then the remaining suffix is solved recursively. The returned lists are copied when combined, so later calls cannot change an earlier result.

```python
from functools import lru_cache

class Solution:
    def partition(self, s: str):
        @lru_cache(None)
        def solve(start):
            if start == len(s):
                return ((),)

            partitions = []
            for end in range(start + 1, len(s) + 1):
                piece = s[start:end]
                if piece == piece[::-1]:
                    for rest in solve(end):
                        partitions.append((piece,) + rest)
            return tuple(partitions)

        return [list(partition) for partition in solve(0)]
```

Dry run for `s = "aab"`: the first call checks `"a"`, `"aa"`, and `"aab"`. The first two are valid prefixes. Solving the suffix after `"a"` gives `["a", "b"]`, while solving the suffix after `"aa"` gives `["b"]`. Combining them produces the two expected partitions.

The empty string reaches the base case and returns `[[]]`. The input is not changed. With memoization, suffix results are computed once, but storing all returned partitions still takes a lot of space.

Time complexity is `O(n^2 * 2^n)` in a simple bound because prefixes and partition lists are copied. Space complexity is `O(n * 2^n)` for the memoized results, excluding temporary slicing details.

This keeps the recursion focused on one index. From `start`, I try every possible ending position. A two-pointer check decides whether that piece can be added to the current path.

After the recursive call, `pop_back()` restores the path so the next possible ending position starts with the correct state. The string itself is only read, not modified.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<string> > partition(string s) {
        vector<vector<string> > answer;
        vector<string> path;
        search(s, 0, path, answer);
        return answer;
    }

private:
    bool isPalindrome(const string& s, int left, int right) {
        while (left < right) {
            if (s[left] != s[right]) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    void search(const string& s, int start, vector<string>& path,
                vector<vector<string> >& answer) {
        if (start == static_cast<int>(s.size())) {
            answer.push_back(path);
            return;
        }

        for (int end = start; end < static_cast<int>(s.size()); end++) {
            if (!isPalindrome(s, start, end)) {
                continue;
            }

            path.push_back(s.substr(start, end - start + 1));
            search(s, end + 1, path, answer);
            path.pop_back();
        }
    }
};
```

A single character always passes the palindrome test, so every non-empty input has at least one result made of single-character strings. For example, `"abc"` produces only `["a", "b", "c"]` because no longer piece reads the same backward.

The worst-case time is `O(n * 2^n)`, since a string such as `"aaaa"` allows many valid cuts and each check can scan part of the string. The recursion path uses `O(n)` extra space, excluding the returned answers.

I used a table to record which parts of the string are palindromes. After that, the DFS only visits valid pieces, so it does not repeat the same character comparisons during every recursive call.

For `s = "aab"`, the table marks `"a"`, `"aa"`, and `"b"` as valid. The search then builds `["a", "a", "b"]` and `["aa", "b"]`.

The input string is not changed. An empty string returns one empty partition.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> partition(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        List<List<String>> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        boolean[][] palindrome = new boolean[n][n];

        for (int length = 1; length <= n; length++) {
            for (int left = 0; left + length <= n; left++) {
                int right = left + length - 1;
                if (s.charAt(left) == s.charAt(right)
                        && (length <= 2 || palindrome[left + 1][right - 1])) {
                    palindrome[left][right] = true;
                }
            }
        }

        buildPartitions(s, 0, palindrome, new ArrayList<>(), answer);
        return answer;
    }

    private void buildPartitions(String s, int start, boolean[][] palindrome,
                                 List<String> path, List<List<String>> answer) {
        if (start == s.length()) {
            answer.add(new ArrayList<>(path));
            return;
        }

        for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {
            if (palindrome[start][end]) {
                path.add(s.substring(start, end + 1));
                buildPartitions(s, end + 1, palindrome, path, answer);
                path.remove(path.size() - 1);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

For `"aab"`, the first choice can be `"a"`. From index 1, `"a"` works and then `"b"` finishes the partition. Backtracking removes the last piece and later tries `"aa"` from index 0.

Time complexity is `O(n^2 + n * 2^n)`, including the table and the returned partitions. Extra space is `O(n^2)` for the table, not counting the output.

The bottom-up version builds answers for suffixes from right to left. `partitions[start]` contains every valid partition of `s[start:]`. When a palindrome `s[start:end]` is found, it is placed before every partition already stored for `end`.

This avoids recursion and makes the result construction visible in the loops. Every substring is checked with two pointers, and the input string is not changed.

```python
class Solution:
    def partition(self, s: str):
        n = len(s)
        partitions = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]
        partitions[n] = [[]]

        for start in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
            for end in range(start + 1, n + 1):
                if self.is_palindrome(s, start, end - 1):
                    piece = s[start:end]
                    for rest in partitions[end]:
                        partitions[start].append([piece] + rest)

        return partitions[0]

    def is_palindrome(self, s: str, left: int, right: int) -> bool:
        while left < right:
            if s[left] != s[right]:
                return False
            left += 1
            right -= 1
        return True
```

For `s = "aab"`, the table starts with `partitions[3] = [[]]`. The last character creates `[["b"]]`. Moving left finds `"a"` and `"aa"`, so the entries for the earlier suffixes can be formed by adding those pieces in front of the stored results.

An empty input leaves `partitions[0]` as `[[]]`, which represents one valid empty partition. A string of repeated characters creates the largest output, so the returned list itself can be exponential.

Filling the palindrome checks takes `O(n^3)` with the two-pointer helper called for every substring. Creating all partitions adds up to `O(n * 2^n)`, so a safe overall bound is `O(n^3 + n * 2^n)`. The DP lists use `O(n * 2^n)` space, including the stored output.

